\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{The radical blocks an unqualified extension theorem}

\p{For #{Q(a,b,c)=a^2-b^2} on #{\mathbb R^3}, with #{e_1,e_2,e_3} the
standard coordinate basis, the radical is #{\mathbb R e_3}. The zero-form
lines #{\mathbb R e_3} and #{\mathbb R(e_1+e_2)} are isometric by
#{e_3\mapsto e_1+e_2}, but this map has no ambient isometric extension. Every
ambient isometry preserves the radical, whereas

##{B_Q(e_1+e_2,e_1)=2,}

so #{e_1+e_2} is not radical. This example lies outside both guards above:
the ambient form is degenerate, and the source line is not regular.}

\p{For the projective-geometric-algebra form
#{Q_{\mathrm{PGA}}(a,b,c)=a^2+b^2}, the positive, negative, and radical
dimensions are #{(2,0,1)}, with radical #{\mathbb R e_3}. Its radical
quotient is a two-dimensional positive-definite space. Quotienting preserves
the positive and negative indices
\leanref/tauceti{QuadraticForm.sigPos_lift_radical}
\leanref/tauceti{QuadraticForm.sigNeg_lift_radical}, but it changes the
quadratic space. It therefore cannot silently replace the original degenerate
Clifford algebra or its motor carrier. Failure of the classical ambient
nondegeneracy hypothesis does not show that extension fails for PGA;
TauCeti's regular-subspace theorem may still apply there.}
